================================================================================
TEST T44973 -- CLI Remove mirror interface
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09 09:33-09:36 NZ (box local; both device clocks set to box local time
          before the run, so device and host stamps are directly comparable)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-6299, objective "remove a configured port mirror and confirm the destination
          port returns to normal operation", 5 steps
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (x960-tb277.rel, build Mon Sep 21 11:25:03 UTC 2026)
          swi_b  sin_x96056-20260921-9
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup; saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- the bare `no mirror interface` is correctly rejected and leaves the mirror
         intact; `no mirror interface <source>` removes it from the configuration, stops the
         frame copying, and returns the destination port to normal switching.
      bare form rejected ................ yes      (% Incomplete command.)
      mirror survives the rejection ..... yes      (show mirror unchanged)
      correct form accepted ............. yes
      removed from running-config ....... yes      (plain access port again)
      frame copying stopped ............. yes      (6 mirrored frames before, 0 after)
      forwarding undisturbed ............ yes      (ping 6/6, 0% loss before and after)
      destination back in switching ..... yes      (LLDP and VLAN-1 broadcast reappear)
CAVEAT:  Proven against a mirror that was demonstrably copying frames beforehand, which
         matters here: defects found elsewhere in this group mean a mirror can be reported
         as Enabled while copying nothing, so "no frames after removal" is only meaningful
         if frames were being copied before.

