STEP 0 -- a working mirror must exist before removal can mean anything    done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n 'icmp'  while pinging 10.38.22.70 from 10.38.22.65
  counted: echo REQUESTS = 6 ; echo REPLIES = 0
  ping: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  <-- the mirror is live and demonstrably copying frames, so a later absence is attributable
      to the removal and not to a mirror that never worked

STEP 1 -- "the bare `no mirror interface` is rejected"                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.10
  % port1.0.10 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
                                            <-- advisory on ENTERING the interface, not a
                                                rejection; the prompt advanced
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface
  % Incomplete command.                     <-- the bare form IS rejected by the parser
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48           <-- UNCHANGED: the rejection removed nothing
  => STEP 1 PASS: `no mirror interface` without a source argument is refused with
     `% Incomplete command.` and the existing mirror survives the attempt intact. The command
     requires an argument -- the CLI offers either an interface range or `none`.

STEP 2 -- "the correct form removes it from the configuration"            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface port1.0.48
  % port1.0.10 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
                                            <-- advisory again; the command succeeded
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
                                            <-- empty
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
  interface port1.0.10
   switchport
   switchport mode access                   <-- mirror line gone, plain access port again
  => STEP 2 PASS: the removal is accepted and leaves no residue in the running-config.

STEP 3 -- "confirm the frame copying actually stopped"                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n 'icmp'  while pinging 10.38.22.70 from 10.38.22.65
  0 packets captured
  0 packets received by filter
  ping: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  counted: echo REQUESTS = 0 ; echo REPLIES = 0
  => STEP 3 PASS: the identical flow that produced 6 mirrored frames at STEP 0 now produces
     none, while still passing end to end. Mirroring stopped in the dataplane, not merely in
     the configuration -- which has to be shown separately on this platform, because the
     configuration display and the dataplane can disagree.

STEP 4 -- "the destination port returned to normal operation"             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a mirror destination leaves every VLAN and stops participating in switching, so
  proving it came back is part of proving the removal. `show interface status` cannot show
  this -- it reports a mirror destination identically with and without a mirror. A capture
  can: while port1.0.10 was a mirror destination, eth3 saw no ordinary VLAN traffic at all.
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n -c 5
  ...
  09:36:11.954256 LLDP, length 50
  5 packets captured
  5 packets received by filter
  => STEP 4 PASS: with the mirror removed, eth3 again receives ordinary VLAN-1 traffic --
     LLDP and broadcast -- which it had not seen while the port was a mirror destination.
     The port is back in normal switching. This is positive proof of a kind the status
     commands are incapable of giving.
